1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,031
Your team keeps four hundred versions of
a 16 MB evaluation set in one repository.

2
00:00:09,071 --> 00:00:13,602
The CTO does the arithmetic on a whiteboard,
four hundred times sixteen megabytes,

3
00:00:13,642 --> 00:00:15,275
and asks: are we storing 6 GB?

4
00:00:15,615 --> 00:00:20,219
You've told people for the whole course
that Git stores snapshots, not diffs.

5
00:00:20,259 --> 00:00:24,924
A snapshot is the complete state of every
tracked file at one moment, so every

6
00:00:24,964 --> 00:00:29,809
commit is a complete tree. If that's true,
the whiteboard arithmetic looks right.

7
00:00:29,849 --> 00:00:34,695
And yet the repository on disk is small.
Both statements have to be true at once,

8
00:00:34,735 --> 00:00:38,012
and this video shows how.
Keep that whiteboard in mind.

9
00:00:38,052 --> 00:00:38,856
It comes back.

10
00:00:40,696 --> 00:00:44,463
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:44,503 --> 00:00:49,018
In the last two videos every object,
every unit of Git's storage, was one

12
00:00:49,058 --> 00:00:52,513
zlib-compressed file under
.git/objects, a loose object.

13
00:00:52,553 --> 00:00:57,380
That's how every object begins. It isn't
how most objects are stored for long.

14
00:00:57,420 --> 00:01:02,372
Maintenance, Git's housekeeping, consolidates
loose objects into a packfile, and

15
00:01:02,412 --> 00:01:07,364
inside a packfile an object may be stored
as a difference from a similar object.

16
00:01:07,704 --> 00:01:14,070
The plan: the concept, then
four replays from labs/ch03.

17
00:01:14,110 --> 00:01:20,362
packfiles.sh and pack-anatomy.sh
are the demonstration.

18
00:01:20,402 --> 00:01:29,513
lab-16-1-loose-to-pack.sh and
lab-16-2-delta-chain.sh are the two labs you'll do

19
00:01:29,553 --> 00:01:34,089
yourself, shown up to their checkpoints.

20
00:01:34,429 --> 00:01:39,591
One command in this video changes state:
git gc, labelled 🟡 CAUTION in the chapter's

21
00:01:39,631 --> 00:01:42,315
command safety table.
Everything else reads.

22
00:01:44,155 --> 00:01:45,567
After this video you can:

23
00:01:45,907 --> 00:01:50,923
Explain how a pack stores many objects
and why that does not change the snapshot

24
00:01:50,963 --> 00:01:51,302
model.

25
00:01:51,642 --> 00:01:54,480
Watch loose objects become
a pack and count both.

26
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:59,332
Read a git verify-pack -v listing and
follow a delta chain to its base.

27
00:01:59,672 --> 00:02:04,177
Say which version of the lab's file is
stored whole and how the listing shows it.

28
00:02:04,517 --> 00:02:06,219
Say what the pack index is for.

29
00:02:08,059 --> 00:02:12,879
Why packs exist: a loose object is one file
per object, and each version of a file

30
00:02:12,919 --> 00:02:17,740
is a complete compressed copy. That's fine
for today's work and wasteful for years

31
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:18,392
of history.

32
00:02:18,732 --> 00:02:23,977
In one sentence: a packfile stores many
objects in one file and may store an object

33
00:02:24,017 --> 00:02:29,198
as a difference from a similar object, an
index file beside it finds any object by

34
00:02:29,238 --> 00:02:32,191
ID, and none of this
changes what an object is.

35
00:02:32,531 --> 00:02:37,088
Now precisely. Every command that
records content writes loose objects.

36
00:02:37,128 --> 00:02:42,074
Maintenance later consolidates them into
a pack. In this video that's git gc.

37
00:02:42,114 --> 00:02:46,995
The automatic strategies are the subject
of Chapter 26 and of a later video.

38
00:02:47,035 --> 00:02:48,419
A pack is three files.

39
00:02:48,759 --> 00:02:54,298
The .pack file has a 12-byte header: the
signature PACK, a version, and the number

40
00:02:54,338 --> 00:02:55,046
of objects.

41
00:02:55,386 --> 00:02:59,610
Then one entry per object, and at the
end a checksum of everything before it.

42
00:02:59,650 --> 00:03:02,490
An entry is a type, a size
and zlib-compressed data.

43
00:03:02,530 --> 00:03:07,086
In the deltified form, the data is a list
of copy and insert instructions against a

44
00:03:07,126 --> 00:03:11,460
base object, and the base is named by its
offset in the same pack or by its ID.

45
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:17,840
The .idx file is the pack index. It holds
the object IDs in sorted order, a CRC32

46
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:23,994
and the pack offset for each, and a 256-entry
table saying where the IDs with each

47
00:03:24,034 --> 00:03:30,074
first byte begin. A CRC32 is a small
checksum, and an offset is a position in the

48
00:03:30,114 --> 00:03:35,853
file. Finding an object is a binary
search in the index, then one read at the

49
00:03:35,893 --> 00:03:36,378
offset.

50
00:03:36,718 --> 00:03:39,743
The .rev file is the reverse map, from
position in the pack to position in the

51
00:03:39,783 --> 00:03:39,979
index.

52
00:03:40,319 --> 00:03:50,315
The manual states the principle in one line.
I'll read it as written: "Conceptually

53
00:03:50,355 --> 00:03:57,691
there are only four object types:
commit, tree, tag and blob.

54
00:03:57,731 --> 00:04:07,244
However to save space, an object could
be stored as a 'delta' of another 'base'

55
00:04:07,284 --> 00:04:08,211
object."

56
00:04:08,551 --> 00:04:13,015
How does Git choose what to compare?
It's a heuristic, a rule of thumb.

57
00:04:13,055 --> 00:04:18,154
git pack-objects sorts the objects by type,
size and name, compares each with its

58
00:04:18,194 --> 00:04:23,482
neighbours inside a window of ten, the
--window option, and doesn't let a chain grow

59
00:04:23,522 --> 00:04:25,893
deeper than fifty, the --depth option.

60
00:04:26,233 --> 00:04:30,811
Three statements keep the two levels apart,
and you should be able to say all three.

61
00:04:31,151 --> 00:04:34,599
First: a delta is between two
objects chosen for similarity.

62
00:04:34,639 --> 00:04:39,250
It's not "the change a commit made". The
base can be another version of the same

63
00:04:39,290 --> 00:04:40,704
file or a different file.

64
00:04:41,044 --> 00:04:46,298
Second: IDs are computed from full content.
So packing changes no ID, no tree and no

65
00:04:46,338 --> 00:04:51,340
commit, and git cat-file, git show and
git checkout always deliver full content.

66
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:56,508
On screen is the oldest version from today's
demo, loose and then packed, with the

67
00:04:56,548 --> 00:04:57,012
same ID.

68
00:04:57,352 --> 00:05:01,829
Third: the choice of base, in the words
of the git cat-file manual, "is arbitrary

69
00:05:01,869 --> 00:05:06,512
and is subject to change during a repack".
So a size on disk says little about which

70
00:05:06,552 --> 00:05:07,572
commit "caused" it.

71
00:05:07,912 --> 00:05:12,465
Inside .git, when git gc runs,
loose object files disappear.

72
00:05:12,505 --> 00:05:17,058
One .pack, one .idx and one .rev
appear under objects/pack/.

73
00:05:17,098 --> 00:05:22,033
And git gc also writes packed-refs
and objects/info/commit-graph.

74
00:05:22,073 --> 00:05:25,096
Both of those come back in later videos.

75
00:05:25,436 --> 00:05:30,769
When does this not help? Three limits.
Deltas need similar bytes, so compressed

76
00:05:30,809 --> 00:05:36,414
archives and model weights gain nothing.
Files above core.bigFileThreshold, 512 MiB

77
00:05:36,454 --> 00:05:40,019
by default, are stored without
any attempt at deltas.

78
00:05:40,059 --> 00:05:45,665
And a clone transfers a pack, so everything
reachable is downloaded however well it

79
00:05:45,705 --> 00:05:46,413
compresses.

80
00:05:48,253 --> 00:05:53,685
The textbook's analogy is a warehouse. It
keeps one complete copy of a long contract

81
00:05:53,725 --> 00:05:59,026
and, for each earlier draft, a sheet of
instructions: copy bytes 0 to 2,400 of the

82
00:05:59,066 --> 00:06:02,674
complete copy, insert these
twelve bytes, copy the rest.

83
00:06:02,714 --> 00:06:07,625
You ask for a draft by its number and
you always receive the complete draft.

84
00:06:07,965 --> 00:06:12,671
Where the analogy breaks is in who decides.
The warehouse chooses which version is

85
00:06:12,711 --> 00:06:16,259
kept whole. It may choose
differently at every reorganisation.

86
00:06:16,299 --> 00:06:18,921
And nothing you see
through Git depends on it.

87
00:06:19,261 --> 00:06:24,400
So keep two levels in your head. The object
level is what Git promises: four types,

88
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:29,517
full content, an ID computed from that
content. The storage level is how the bytes

89
00:06:29,557 --> 00:06:34,072
happen to sit on disk today. The
snapshot model lives at the first level.

90
00:06:34,112 --> 00:06:35,695
Deltas live at the second.

91
00:06:36,035 --> 00:06:40,562
Before I draw it, a quick quiz. Six
versions of one file sit in a pack.

92
00:06:40,602 --> 00:06:44,872
Which one is kept whole: the oldest
or the newest? Say it out loud.

93
00:06:51,212 --> 00:06:58,742
Six versions of one file. In the first row,
a4e58256, stored whole: 1,372 bytes in

94
00:06:58,782 --> 00:07:06,405
the pack for an object of 16,789 bytes.
Then 739088cd at depth 1, stored as a delta

95
00:07:06,445 --> 00:07:13,975
against it, 35 bytes in the pack. Then
depth 2, 3, 4, and in the last row 0928d48c

96
00:07:14,015 --> 00:07:14,990
at depth 5.

97
00:07:15,330 --> 00:07:19,833
Now read the first row again. The newest
version is the whole one, and the old

98
00:07:19,873 --> 00:07:24,142
versions are the deltas. If you said
the oldest, that's the natural guess.

99
00:07:24,182 --> 00:07:27,578
It's the opposite of what "Git
stores diffs" would suggest.

100
00:07:27,618 --> 00:07:31,713
To read the oldest version, Git reads
the base and applies five deltas.

101
00:07:32,053 --> 00:07:36,110
Read the last column. As an object,
every version has its full size.

102
00:07:36,150 --> 00:07:40,869
That column is what Git shows you. The
column before it is what the disk holds.

103
00:07:45,209 --> 00:07:48,945
An evaluation harness whose
test-case file has 200 lines.

104
00:07:48,985 --> 00:07:51,594
Five commits each change one line of it.

105
00:07:51,934 --> 00:07:55,909
Six commits. Predict the count
line: how many loose objects?

106
00:07:55,949 --> 00:07:59,523
Count the commits, the trees
and the blobs. I'll wait.

107
00:08:04,363 --> 00:08:08,932
Twenty-five: six commits, twelve trees,
six versions of cases.jsonl and one small

108
00:08:08,972 --> 00:08:13,713
configuration file. count and size describe
loose objects, and size is disk space in

109
00:08:13,753 --> 00:08:18,323
KiB. Every one of the 25 files occupies a
4 KiB block, however small its content.

110
00:08:18,663 --> 00:08:24,429
Now the 🟡 command. git gc packs objects and
refs, expires reflog entries by age, and

111
00:08:24,469 --> 00:08:30,236
deletes unreachable objects older than two
weeks. A reflog is the local journal of a

112
00:08:30,276 --> 00:08:35,351
ref's past values, and an unreachable
object is one that no name leads to.

113
00:08:35,391 --> 00:08:39,291
Preview what it could remove
with git fsck --unreachable.

114
00:08:39,331 --> 00:08:42,471
Within the retention
periods, nothing is lost.

115
00:08:42,811 --> 00:08:45,885
What will count say now,
and what will in-pack say?

116
00:08:45,925 --> 00:08:46,861
Say it out loud.

117
00:08:51,701 --> 00:08:55,872
The same 25 objects, now in-pack,
in 4 KiB where there were 100.

118
00:08:55,912 --> 00:08:59,951
Look at the three pack files,
and at commit-graph beside them.

119
00:09:00,291 --> 00:09:01,443
Look inside the pack.

120
00:09:01,783 --> 00:09:07,078
The columns are object ID, type,
size, size in the pack, and offset.

121
00:09:07,118 --> 00:09:13,276
A deltified object has two more: the depth
of its chain and the ID of its base.

122
00:09:13,316 --> 00:09:19,631
Find the line for a4e58256: 16,789 bytes
that compress to 1,372, and five columns

123
00:09:19,671 --> 00:09:21,749
only, so it's stored whole.

124
00:09:22,089 --> 00:09:26,080
Find 739088cd: seven columns,
depth 1, base a4e58256.

125
00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:30,186
For a delta, the size column
is the size of the delta.

126
00:09:30,226 --> 00:09:36,421
The summary at the bottom counts chain
lengths: twenty objects that aren't deltas,

127
00:09:36,461 --> 00:09:39,615
and one object at each length from 1 to 5.

128
00:09:39,955 --> 00:09:42,548
Is the oldest version
still a complete file?

129
00:09:42,888 --> 00:09:47,042
A quick quiz. The oldest version
costs 35 bytes in the pack.

130
00:09:47,082 --> 00:09:52,356
How many lines will git cat-file -p
print for it: one, a handful, or all two

131
00:09:52,396 --> 00:09:53,824
hundred? Your answer?

132
00:09:58,664 --> 00:10:04,144
Two hundred lines. %(objectsize) is the
size of the object, %(objectsize:disk) is

133
00:10:04,184 --> 00:10:09,732
what its entry costs in the pack, and
%(deltabase) is all zeros for an object that

134
00:10:09,772 --> 00:10:10,823
is stored whole.

135
00:10:11,163 --> 00:10:14,179
One more step. Commit
again after the git gc.

136
00:10:14,519 --> 00:10:19,783
Four loose objects next to the pack of 25.
New commits are loose objects again until

137
00:10:19,823 --> 00:10:24,330
the next consolidation. That's the
normal state of a working repository.

138
00:10:24,670 --> 00:10:35,972
Try it now, thirty seconds, in a repository
in the lab shell: run git count-objects

139
00:10:36,012 --> 00:10:41,575
-v and read count and in-pack. I'll wait.

140
00:10:46,415 --> 00:10:49,208
count is the loose objects,
in-pack the packed ones.

141
00:10:49,248 --> 00:10:51,878
Now you can tell which
storage you're looking at.

142
00:10:54,718 --> 00:10:59,717
Twelve bytes: the signature PACK, version
2, and hexadecimal 19, which is 25

143
00:10:59,757 --> 00:11:05,219
objects. The last twenty bytes are the
checksum of everything before them, and that

144
00:11:05,259 --> 00:11:07,738
checksum is also the name of the file.

145
00:11:08,078 --> 00:11:10,740
The index starts with a
magic number and version 2.

146
00:11:10,780 --> 00:11:14,183
Its entries are offset, object ID
and CRC32, sorted by object ID.

147
00:11:14,223 --> 00:11:18,473
Sorted by offset, the same entries give
the order of objects inside the pack: 12,

148
00:11:18,513 --> 00:11:22,817
175, 341, the offsets the pack listing
showed. The index ends with the checksum of

149
00:11:22,857 --> 00:11:27,213
its pack, then a checksum of itself. The
index is derived data: Lab 16.1 deletes it

150
00:11:27,253 --> 00:11:28,803
and rebuilds an identical one.

151
00:11:29,143 --> 00:11:33,878
A vocabulary file of 400 tokens
and three commits. After git gc:

152
00:11:42,018 --> 00:11:47,089
Look at the three blob lines. One is
whole, and two have seven columns, both at

153
00:11:47,129 --> 00:11:52,134
depth 1 against the same base. The first
version of the file still has its 400

154
00:11:52,174 --> 00:11:52,523
lines.

155
00:12:00,863 --> 00:12:04,885
The chain, shallowest first, and then
the mapping from object to commit.

156
00:12:04,925 --> 00:12:07,876
The object stored whole
belongs to the newest commit.

157
00:12:07,916 --> 00:12:11,939
Stop here: the rest of each lab, the
failure and the recovery, is yours.

158
00:12:13,779 --> 00:12:15,427
Five mistakes to watch for.

159
00:12:15,767 --> 00:12:19,111
Saying "Git stores diffs"
after seeing a pack listing.

160
00:12:19,151 --> 00:12:24,187
Root cause: a delta is a storage detail
between two similar objects; every object

161
00:12:24,227 --> 00:12:28,448
still has its full content and its
ID is computed from that content.

162
00:12:28,788 --> 00:12:32,852
Blaming a commit for disk usage
because its blob is the large entry.

163
00:12:32,892 --> 00:12:37,258
Root cause: the choice of base is
arbitrary and can change at any repack.

164
00:12:37,598 --> 00:12:41,482
Expecting packs to shrink model
weights or compressed archives.

165
00:12:41,522 --> 00:12:43,848
Root cause: deltas need similar bytes.

166
00:12:44,188 --> 00:12:50,151
Treating a missing .idx as lost data. Root
cause: the pack index is derived from the

167
00:12:50,191 --> 00:12:53,296
pack and can be rebuilt
with git index-pack.

168
00:12:53,636 --> 00:12:58,597
Being surprised by loose objects after
git gc. Root cause: every command that

169
00:12:58,637 --> 00:13:02,884
records content writes loose objects;
consolidation happens later.

170
00:13:04,724 --> 00:13:09,172
Now, out of the lab, and back to the
whiteboard. Four hundred versions of a

171
00:13:09,212 --> 00:13:13,959
line-oriented 16 MB evaluation set that
differ in a few records cost roughly one

172
00:13:13,999 --> 00:13:18,866
compressed copy plus four hundred small
deltas. That's the answer for a JSON Lines

173
00:13:18,906 --> 00:13:23,773
file of test cases. The whiteboard was
right about the objects and wrong about the

174
00:13:23,813 --> 00:13:24,072
disk.

175
00:13:24,412 --> 00:13:28,998
The same team also commits a model
checkpoint. For that file the answer is

176
00:13:29,038 --> 00:13:34,250
different, and you can say why before you
measure. Deltas need similar bytes, so the

177
00:13:34,290 --> 00:13:38,939
checkpoint gains nothing. Above
core.bigFileThreshold Git doesn't even try.

178
00:13:38,979 --> 00:13:44,065
And whatever the compression, a clone
transfers a pack, so everything reachable is

179
00:13:44,105 --> 00:13:47,379
downloaded. That's where
Chapter 22, Git LFS, begins.

180
00:13:49,219 --> 00:13:55,480
Your turn. Do Lab 16.1, "Watch
loose objects become a pack", in

181
00:13:55,520 --> 00:14:03,881
lab-manual/m16-object-database.md. Before
you run git gc, predict count, in-pack and

182
00:14:03,921 --> 00:14:12,082
packs. Before the fourth commit, predict
what git count-objects -v will show after

183
00:14:12,122 --> 00:14:17,283
it. Then do the lab's failure
scenario and recovery.

184
00:14:17,623 --> 00:14:22,685
The challenge is Lab 16.2, "Read a pack
listing and find a delta chain", in the same

185
00:14:22,725 --> 00:14:27,424
file. Before you read the listing, predict
which version of the file is stored

186
00:14:27,464 --> 00:14:27,788
whole.

187
00:14:29,628 --> 00:14:32,453
Question 77 of the CTO question bank:

188
00:14:32,793 --> 00:14:37,280
A strong answer separates the object
level from the storage level in its first

189
00:14:37,320 --> 00:14:42,098
sentence. It says what determines whether
deltas are possible at all, describes the

190
00:14:42,138 --> 00:14:46,857
direction of the chain and what follows
for every object that depends on the base,

191
00:14:46,897 --> 00:14:51,442
and ends with how the damage is detected
and from where the object is restored.

192
00:14:51,482 --> 00:14:55,156
Lab 16.2 lets you produce the
damage yourself before you answer.

193
00:14:56,996 --> 00:15:01,929
Let's land this. Two levels: what Git
promises, and how the bytes sit on disk today.

194
00:15:02,269 --> 00:15:20,985
You should now be able to say:

195
00:15:21,325 --> 00:15:26,900
New objects are loose; maintenance consolidates
them into a .pack with an .idx and a

196
00:15:26,940 --> 00:15:27,235
.rev.

197
00:15:27,575 --> 00:15:32,416
In a pack, an object may be stored as a
delta against a similar base, chosen by a

198
00:15:32,456 --> 00:15:33,019
heuristic.

199
00:15:33,359 --> 00:15:37,744
Packing changes no ID: every object
reads back with its full content.

200
00:15:38,084 --> 00:15:43,235
In a git verify-pack -v listing, seven
columns mean a delta, and the last two are

201
00:15:43,275 --> 00:15:44,196
depth and base.

202
00:15:44,536 --> 00:15:47,746
The pack index maps IDs to
offsets and can be rebuilt.

203
00:15:49,586 --> 00:15:57,726
Read section 3.7 of Chapter 3. Do Exercise
16.3, "Loose objects become a pack", in

204
00:15:57,766 --> 00:16:00,818
exercises/m16-m18-internals.md.

205
00:16:01,158 --> 00:16:04,846
Today you looked inside a pack,
and the snapshot model survived.

206
00:16:04,886 --> 00:16:09,621
Do the lab before the next video: reachability,
git fsck, and where Git checks its

207
00:16:09,661 --> 00:16:13,116
hashes. Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

208
00:16:13,156 --> 00:16:14,514
See you in the next one.
